-- [Titration & Titration Curves] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A 25.00 mL sample of a weak monoprotic acid (0.100 M) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH, and pH is recorded versus volume of base added. The equivalence point occurs at 25.00 mL, where the pH is 8.72. After 12.50 mL of base has been added, the recorded pH is 4.74. Based on these data, what is the \(\text{p}K_\text{a}\) of the acid?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "4.74"}, {"label": "B", "text": "2.87"}, {"label": "C", "text": "8.72"}, {"label": "D", "text": "9.26"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Titration (GC).'' On a weak-acid/strong-base titration curve, the half-equivalence point is the volume at which exactly half of the acid has been neutralized, leaving equal concentrations of the weak acid and its conjugate base. By the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_\text{a} + \log\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\), so when \([\text{A}^-] = [\text{HA}]\) the log term is zero and \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_\text{a}\). (Choice A) is correct: the equivalence point is at 25.00 mL, so the half-equivalence point is at 12.50 mL, where the recorded pH is 4.74; because \([\text{A}^-] = [\text{HA}]\) there, \(\text{p}K_\text{a} = 4.74\). (Choice B) is incorrect: 2.87 would be the initial pH of the acid before any base is added, which reflects the acid''s starting ionization and concentration, not the buffer condition where \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_\text{a}\). (Choice C) is incorrect: 8.72 is the pH at the equivalence point, which is basic because the solution now contains only the conjugate base; the equivalence-point pH depends on the conjugate base''s hydrolysis and dilution, not on \(\text{p}K_\text{a}\) directly. (Choice D) is incorrect: 9.26 is \(14 - 4.74\), the value obtained by confusing \(\text{p}K_\text{a}\) with \(\text{p}K_\text{b}\); the half-equivalence pH gives \(\text{p}K_\text{a}\) of the acid directly, not \(\text{p}K_\text{b}\) of its conjugate base. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the half-equivalence-point relationship to titration-curve data to extract \(\text{p}K_\text{a}\).',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0eef9186-e67e-4914-87b5-d37ae21984db';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0eef9186-e67e-4914-87b5-d37ae21984db';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0eef9186-e67e-4914-87b5-d37ae21984db', 'B', 'misconception', 'reads initial pH instead of half-equivalence pH'),
  ('0eef9186-e67e-4914-87b5-d37ae21984db', 'C', 'misconception', 'reads equivalence-point pH as pKa'),
  ('0eef9186-e67e-4914-87b5-d37ae21984db', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses pKa with pKb (14 - pKa)');

-- [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Aspartate is titrated from low to high pH and shows three ionizations with \(\text{pK}_a\) values of \(2.0\) (\(\alpha\)-carboxyl), \(3.9\) (side-chain carboxyl), and \(9.9\) (\(\alpha\)-amino). Which value best estimates the isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\)) of aspartate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(3.0\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(6.0\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(6.9\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(5.3\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules.'' The isoelectric point is the pH at which a molecule carries no net charge, and for an amino acid it equals the average of the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank the species whose net charge is zero. As pH rises, aspartate loses protons in order of increasing \(\text{pK}_a\): the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(2.0\)), then the side-chain carboxyl (\(3.9\)), then the \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(9.9\)); the net charge passes from \(+1\) to \(0\) to \(-1\) to \(-2\). The neutral species is bracketed by the two lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) values. (Choice A) is correct: because aspartate is acidic, the neutral form sits between the two carboxyl ionizations, so \(\text{pI} = (2.0 + 3.9)/2 \approx 3.0\), which correctly falls in the acidic range expected for an amino acid with two acidic groups. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(6.0\) is the average of the highest and lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.0 + 9.9)/2 \approx 6.0\); this rule works for a neutral amino acid like glycine but fails here because the neutral species of aspartate is not flanked by the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl and \(\alpha\)-amino groups. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(6.9\) is the average of the two highest \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((3.9 + 9.9)/2\); those two ionizations bracket the species with net charge \(-1\), not the neutral species, so this overestimates \(\text{pI}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: \(5.3\) is approximately the mean of all three \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.0 + 3.9 + 9.9)/3 \approx 5.3\); \(\text{pI}\) is never computed by averaging every \(\text{pK}_a\), only the two that flank the zero-charge form. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of the isoelectric point to determine which two of three \(\text{pK}_a\) values flank the neutral species and average them.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8874aae1-5b1f-4156-a97f-66661d3ac956';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8874aae1-5b1f-4156-a97f-66661d3ac956';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8874aae1-5b1f-4156-a97f-66661d3ac956', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies the glycine (lowest+highest pKa) pI rule to an acidic amino acid'),
  ('8874aae1-5b1f-4156-a97f-66661d3ac956', 'C', 'misconception', 'averages the two pKa flanking the wrong (charged) species'),
  ('8874aae1-5b1f-4156-a97f-66661d3ac956', 'D', 'misconception', 'averages all three pKa values instead of the two flanking neutral');

-- [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Bicarbonate \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) is amphiprotic and can function on either side of a Brønsted–Lowry reaction. In one such reaction, a bicarbonate ion accepts a proton from a neighboring species. Which species is the conjugate acid of bicarbonate that results from this reaction?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{H}_2\\text{CO}_3\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{CO}_3^{2-}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{OH}^-\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' In Brønsted–Lowry terms, an amphiprotic species such as bicarbonate can either donate or accept a proton; when it accepts a proton it acts as a base, and the textbook states that when a base accepts \(\text{H}^+\) it is converted into its conjugate acid. The key inference is that this reaction runs in the protonation direction (charge becomes one unit less negative), not the deprotonation direction that bicarbonate more familiarly undergoes. (Choice A) is correct: accepting one proton adds \(\text{H}^+\) to \(\text{HCO}_3^-\), raising the charge from \(-1\) to \(0\) and giving carbonic acid, \(\text{HCO}_3^- + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), which is the conjugate acid of bicarbonate. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) is the conjugate base of bicarbonate, formed when bicarbonate instead donates a proton; this is the reverse of the process described, and the stem specifies that bicarbonate accepts a proton. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{H}_3\text{O}^+\) is the conjugate acid of water, produced when water accepts a proton; it is a plausible product elsewhere in the system but is not derived from bicarbonate, so it cannot be bicarbonate''s conjugate acid. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{OH}^-\) would form if bicarbonate acted on water, but a conjugate acid must be the original species with one added proton, so the correct product must retain the carbon and all three oxygens of bicarbonate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of a conjugate acid to bicarbonate acting in its less-familiar basic role and determine the correct protonation product rather than reflexively deprotonating it.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0b774690-dd8e-4576-9070-fb79f1667309';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0b774690-dd8e-4576-9070-fb79f1667309';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0b774690-dd8e-4576-9070-fb79f1667309', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'conjugate base instead of conjugate acid'),
  ('0b774690-dd8e-4576-9070-fb79f1667309', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'conjugate acid of water, not of the named species'),
  ('0b774690-dd8e-4576-9070-fb79f1667309', 'D', 'misconception', 'base must produce hydroxide (Arrhenius overreach)');

-- [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During intense sprinting, lactic acid pours free \(\text{H}^+\) into a runner''s blood faster than the lungs or kidneys can react. Considering only the bicarbonate equilibrium \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\) in the first seconds, before any organ compensation, what happens to the concentrations of \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) and dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The equilibrium shifts left; \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) falls and dissolved \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) rises."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The equilibrium shifts right; \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) rises and dissolved \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) rises."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The equilibrium shifts right; \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) falls and dissolved \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) falls."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The equilibrium shifts left; \\(\\text{HCO}_3^-\\) rises and dissolved \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) falls."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems.'' The textbook describes blood as a buffered solution whose buffering action comes from the carbonic acid / bicarbonate pair, in which an added hydronium ion is removed primarily by combining with \(\text{HCO}_3^-\). Applying Le Chatelier''s principle, adding the product \(\text{H}^+\) to the right side of \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{CO}_3 \rightleftharpoons \text{H}^+ + \text{HCO}_3^-\) drives the system toward the reactant side, so you must track that leftward shift through to both the bicarbonate and the CO2 sides. (Choice A) is correct: the added \(\text{H}^+\) combines with \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) to form \(\text{H}_2\text{CO}_3\), which proceeds to \(\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}\); the leftward shift therefore consumes bicarbonate (its concentration falls) while generating dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\) (its concentration rises), which the lungs later exhale. (Choice B) is incorrect: a rightward shift would generate still more \(\text{H}^+\), adding to the very stress just imposed instead of relieving it, so this reverses the actual Le Chatelier direction even though it correctly predicts CO2 rising. (Choice C) is incorrect: it also invokes an impossible rightward (H+-producing) shift, and a right shift would consume rather than release \(\text{CO}_2\), so both concentration predictions are wrong. (Choice D) is incorrect: the shift is indeed leftward, but \(\text{HCO}_3^-\) is consumed as it mops up the added acid, so its concentration falls rather than rises, and dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\) is produced rather than depleted; rising bicarbonate reflects the later renal regeneration of buffer, not this immediate equilibrium response. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Le Chatelier''s principle to a multi-step equilibrium and trace how an added product changes concentrations on both the bicarbonate and dissolved-CO2 ends of the reaction.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7041404f-ba58-43b5-b78a-ccc2c8dbc811';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7041404f-ba58-43b5-b78a-ccc2c8dbc811';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7041404f-ba58-43b5-b78a-ccc2c8dbc811', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'shift-toward-added-stress'),
  ('7041404f-ba58-43b5-b78a-ccc2c8dbc811', 'C', 'misconception', 'buffer-neutralizes-by-shifting-right'),
  ('7041404f-ba58-43b5-b78a-ccc2c8dbc811', 'D', 'misconception', 'bicarbonate-accumulates-to-absorb-acid');

-- [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist compiles acid-ionization constants for four monoprotic weak acids at 25°C: hydrofluoric acid \((\text{HF}, K_a = 6.4 \times 10^{-4})\), acetic acid \((\text{CH}_3\text{CO}_2\text{H}, K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5})\), hypochlorous acid \((\text{HClO}, K_a = 2.9 \times 10^{-8})\), and hydrocyanic acid \((\text{HCN}, K_a = 4.9 \times 10^{-10})\). Of the conjugate bases of these four acids, which is the strongest base?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{F}^-\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{CO}_2^-\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{ClO}^-\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{CN}^-\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' For any conjugate acid-base pair, the ionization constants are related by \(K_a \times K_b = K_w\), so the two constants are inversely proportional: the weaker the acid (smaller \(K_a\)), the stronger its conjugate base (larger \(K_b\)). To find the strongest conjugate base, identify the acid with the smallest \(K_a\); its conjugate base has the largest \(K_b\). Among the four acids, HCN has the smallest \(K_a\) \((4.9 \times 10^{-10})\), making it the weakest acid. (Choice D) is correct: because HCN is the weakest of the four acids, its conjugate base, \(\text{CN}^-\), has the largest \(K_b\) and is therefore the strongest base. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{F}^-\) is the conjugate base of HF, which has the largest \(K_a\) \((6.4 \times 10^{-4})\); as the conjugate base of the strongest acid, \(\text{F}^-\) is the weakest base, not the strongest. This reverses the inverse relationship between \(K_a\) and \(K_b\). (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{CH}_3\text{CO}_2^-\) is the conjugate base of acetic acid \((K_a = 1.8 \times 10^{-5})\); because acetic acid is more ionized than HCN, its conjugate base is weaker than \(\text{CN}^-\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{ClO}^-\) is the conjugate base of hypochlorous acid \((K_a = 2.9 \times 10^{-8})\), which is a stronger acid than HCN, so \(\text{ClO}^-\) is a weaker base than \(\text{CN}^-\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the reciprocal relationship \(K_a K_b = K_w\) to novel ionization data to rank conjugate base strengths.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4ca82479-b4ea-44f7-80fd-1a7329fdf984';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4ca82479-b4ea-44f7-80fd-1a7329fdf984';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4ca82479-b4ea-44f7-80fd-1a7329fdf984', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'picks the conjugate base of the strongest acid, inverting the Ka-Kb inverse relationship'),
  ('4ca82479-b4ea-44f7-80fd-1a7329fdf984', 'B', 'misconception', 'anchors on the most familiar weak acid (acetic) rather than the weakest acid in the set'),
  ('4ca82479-b4ea-44f7-80fd-1a7329fdf984', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'picks an intermediate-Ka acid''s conjugate base, stopping one step short of the smallest Ka');

-- [Autoionization, Kw & the pH/pOH Scales] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The autoionization of water is endothermic, so its ion-product constant \(K_w\) is larger at \(37^\circ\text{C}\) (normal body temperature) than the value of \(1.0\times10^{-14}\) it has at \(25^\circ\text{C}\). Which statement correctly describes a sample of pure water warmed to \(37^\circ\text{C}\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its pH is below 7, yet the water remains neutral because \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) still equals \\([\\text{OH}^-]\\)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its pH is below 7, and the water is now acidic because \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) exceeds \\([\\text{OH}^-]\\)."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its pH is still exactly 7, because pure water is neutral at every temperature."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Its pH is above 7, because warming shifts the equilibrium so that \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) decreases."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' Because water''s autoionization is endothermic, raising the temperature shifts the equilibrium toward products, so \(K_w\) grows and both \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and \([\text{OH}^-]\) rise. In pure water the reaction produces the two ions in a 1:1 ratio, so \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = [\text{OH}^-]\) at any temperature, which is the definition of a neutral solution; a lower pH follows only because the larger \(K_w\) drives both ion concentrations above \(1.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\). (Choice A) is correct: with \(K_w\) larger than \(1.0\times10^{-14}\), \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]>1.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\) gives a pH below 7, but because the ions are still generated in equal amounts the water stays neutral. (Choice B) is incorrect: a solution is acidic only when \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]>[\text{OH}^-]\); heating pure water raises both ions equally, so it is neutral, not acidic, despite the sub-7 pH. (Choice C) is incorrect: pH 7 marks neutrality only at \(25^\circ\text{C}\); neutrality is defined by equal ion concentrations, and as \(K_w\) increases the neutral pH drops below 7 (about 6.8 near body temperature). (Choice D) is incorrect: because autoionization is endothermic, warming shifts the equilibrium toward the ions and increases \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\), lowering pH rather than raising it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Le Châtelier''s principle and the temperature dependence of \(K_w\) to distinguish a change in pH from a change in acid-base classification.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'aa10ef0e-198f-4564-9750-04e09e69b653';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'aa10ef0e-198f-4564-9750-04e09e69b653';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('aa10ef0e-198f-4564-9750-04e09e69b653', 'B', 'misconception', 'low pH always means acidic'),
  ('aa10ef0e-198f-4564-9750-04e09e69b653', 'C', 'misconception', 'pure water is always pH 7'),
  ('aa10ef0e-198f-4564-9750-04e09e69b653', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'wrong direction of endothermic shift');

-- [Strong vs Weak Acids/Bases; Ka and Kb] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist has a \(0.10\,\text{M}\) aqueous solution of acetic acid (a weak acid, \(K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\)) at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\). She dilutes it with water to a final concentration of \(0.010\,\text{M}\). Upon dilution, how do the percent ionization of the acid and the hydronium ion concentration change?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Percent ionization increases and \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) decreases."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Percent ionization increases and \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) increases."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Percent ionization decreases and \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) decreases."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Percent ionization stays the same and \\([\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+]\\) decreases."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' For a weak acid only a small fraction of molecules ionize, and the equilibrium \(\text{HA} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_3\text{O}^+ + \text{A}^-\) shifts when the solution is diluted. Because \(K_a\) is fixed at a given temperature, adding water shifts the equilibrium toward the products (a larger ionized fraction), yet the ions produced are spread through a much larger volume, so the actual hydronium concentration still falls. (Choice A) is correct: as the textbook notes, the percent ionization of a weak acid typically increases as the acid is diluted; here it rises from about \(1.3\%\) at \(0.10\,\text{M}\) to about \(4.2\%\) at \(0.010\,\text{M}\), while \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) falls from roughly \(1.3\times10^{-3}\,\text{M}\) to \(4.2\times10^{-4}\,\text{M}\). (Choice B) is incorrect: although the percent ionized does increase, the larger fraction cannot compensate for the tenfold dilution, so the actual hydronium concentration drops rather than rises. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the true trend—percent ionization increases, not decreases, on dilution, even though the student correctly senses that \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) falls. (Choice D) is incorrect: a constant \(K_a\) does not mean a constant percent ionization; unlike \(K_a\), percent ionization depends on the initial concentration and changes as the solution is diluted. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the weak-acid ionization equilibrium to a novel dilution scenario, distinguishing the ionized fraction from the absolute hydronium concentration.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd1fd8c9c-1062-486a-9edf-0c03f566e77e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd1fd8c9c-1062-486a-9edf-0c03f566e77e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d1fd8c9c-1062-486a-9edf-0c03f566e77e', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates a rising ionized fraction with a rising absolute ion concentration'),
  ('d1fd8c9c-1062-486a-9edf-0c03f566e77e', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'believes dilution decreases percent ionization'),
  ('d1fd8c9c-1062-486a-9edf-0c03f566e77e', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes a constant Ka forces a constant percent ionization');

-- [Titration & Titration Curves] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A 25.0 mL sample of 0.10 M acetic acid (\(\text{p}K_a = 4.74\)) is titrated with 0.10 M NaOH, requiring 25.0 mL of titrant to reach the equivalence point. A researcher wants to draw a small volume from the titration flask at the moment when the mixture can absorb the most added acid or base with the least change in \(\text{pH}\). At which volume of added NaOH should the sample be drawn?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "0.0 mL of NaOH added"}, {"label": "B", "text": "12.5 mL of NaOH added"}, {"label": "C", "text": "25.0 mL of NaOH added"}, {"label": "D", "text": "37.5 mL of NaOH added"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Titration (GC).'' Buffer capacity—the amount of acid or base a solution can absorb before its \(\text{pH}\) changes significantly—is greatest when the solution contains comparable amounts of a weak acid and its conjugate base. During the titration of acetic acid with NaOH, added \(\text{OH}^-\) converts \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) into \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\), and by \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\frac{[\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-]}{[\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}]}\) the mixture resists \(\text{pH}\) change most strongly when the log term is near zero, i.e., when the two species are roughly equal. Because the equivalence point falls at 25.0 mL, that equal-concentration condition (the half-equivalence point) occurs at half that volume. (Choice B) is correct: at 12.5 mL, half of the acetic acid has been neutralized, so \([\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}] \approx [\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-]\) and \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a\); with appreciable amounts of both conjugate partners present, the solution absorbs added acid or base with minimal \(\text{pH}\) change, the defining condition of maximum buffer capacity. (Choice A) is incorrect: at 0.0 mL the flask holds essentially pure acetic acid with almost no acetate, so the conjugate pair is far from balanced; the first increments of base then shift the log ratio steeply and the \(\text{pH}\) rises relatively fast, meaning little buffering. (Choice C) is incorrect: at 25.0 mL (the equivalence point) the acetic acid is nearly exhausted and the solution is essentially acetate ion, so there is negligible weak acid left to neutralize added base—this is where \(\text{pH}\) changes most sharply, the region of least buffering. (Choice D) is incorrect: past 25.0 mL the \(\text{pH}\) is set by excess strong NaOH rather than by the acetic acid/acetate pair; the curve is shallow there only because \(\text{pH}\) is logarithmic in \([\text{OH}^-]\), not because the acetate system is buffering. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the buffer-capacity concept and the half-equivalence relationship to locate the titrant volume at which the conjugate pair is balanced.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
