UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist prepares two separate aqueous solutions at 25C: a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution of hydrobromic acid (\(\text{HBr}\), a strong acid) and a \(0.10\ \text{M}\) solution of acetic acid (\(\text{CH}_3\text{CO}_2\text{H}\), \(K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\)). Which statement correctly describes the pH of these two solutions?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The \\(\\text{HBr}\\) solution has a pH of \\(1.00\\), and the acetic acid solution has a higher pH."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both solutions have a pH of \\(1.00\\) because they are prepared at the same concentration."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The acetic acid solution has a pH of \\(1.00\\), and the \\(\\text{HBr}\\) solution has a higher pH."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The \\(\\text{HBr}\\) solution has a pH of \\(1.00\\), and the acetic acid solution has a lower pH."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' Because a strong acid ionizes completely in water while a weak acid ionizes only partially, two acids at the same formal concentration do not produce the same hydronium ion concentration; the stronger acid yields a higher \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and therefore a lower pH. (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{HBr}\) is a strong acid, so it ionizes completely and \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 0.10\ \text{M}\), giving \(\text{pH} = -\log(0.10) = 1.00\); acetic acid is weak, so only a small fraction of it ionizes, leaving \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) well below \(0.10\ \text{M}\) (about \(1.3\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\), pH \(\approx 2.9\)), which is a higher pH than the strong acid. (Choice B) is incorrect: equal formal concentration does not mean equal pH, because pH depends on the concentration of hydronium ion actually produced, which is governed by the extent of ionization rather than the amount of acid dissolved. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the strengths, treating acetic acid as the fully ionized species; only the strong acid \(\text{HBr}\) reaches \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 0.10\ \text{M}\) and pH \(1.00\), while the weak acid falls short. (Choice D) is incorrect: a weak acid produces fewer hydronium ions than a strong acid at the same concentration, so its pH is higher, not lower; assigning it a lower pH implies it ionizes more completely than \(\text{HBr}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between complete and partial ionization to compare the hydronium ion concentrations, and hence the pH values, of a strong and a weak acid at identical formal concentration.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
